WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:05.530
Close your eyes and imagine a small, tipsy
man in green, hoarding gold at the end of

00:00:05.530 --> 00:00:06.710
a rainbow.

00:00:06.710 --> 00:00:09.700
You know this guy, he’s a leprechaun.

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:11.860
&nbsp;
The modern leprechaun -- the diminutive green

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:17.620
figure with a jovial smile, red beard, jaunty
hat and a pot of gold, unfairly associated

00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:23.340
with St. Patrick's Day -- is practically ubiquitous
as a symbol of luck and mischief in Western

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:24.380
pop culture.

00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:30.100
He’s the official mascot of sports teams,
the subject of a ridiculous slasher horror

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:34.660
series, and the face of a deliciously sugary
cereal.

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:40.590
And trust me, you know you've gone mainstream
when you've achieved the status of spokesmonster!

00:00:40.590 --> 00:00:42.730
&nbsp;
But the traditional Leprechaun is a fairy

00:00:42.730 --> 00:00:47.720
rooted in Irish lore known for his skills
as an industrious craftsman—and a bit more

00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:50.020
threatening than you might realize.

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:54.830
He is tied to the ancient mythology of Ireland
and the real history of Irish immigrants to

00:00:54.830 --> 00:00:56.640
the United States.

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:02.649
And yes, he's also a mischievous little creature
with a penchant for gold.

00:01:02.649 --> 00:01:06.790
&nbsp;
[Monstrum intro.]

00:01:06.790 --> 00:01:11.780
While many modern Gaelic speakers prefer to
use the spelling leipreachán, “leprechaun”

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:14.700
is the more common version outside of Ireland.

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:20.180
Leipreachán is an anglicized version of one
of the many Celtic versions of the name.

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:21.860
Which one in particular?

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:27.340
Well, there are a couple of contenders since
this guy has a LOT of names.

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:32.689
That’s because different regions in Ireland
all call this fairy creature something distinctive.&nbsp;

00:01:32.689 --> 00:01:35.700
&nbsp;
Despite the variety of names across the country,

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:38.590
traditional Leprechaun traits remain consistent.

00:01:38.590 --> 00:01:43.340
He is a “solitary” fairy, rarely appearing
with others of his kind or associating with

00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:45.820
other fairy species socially.

00:01:45.820 --> 00:01:51.650
He is always old, wizened, oddly attired and
very small—ranging from a few inches to

00:01:51.650 --> 00:01:53.320
a couple of feet tall.

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:55.430
He’s always male.

00:01:55.430 --> 00:01:57.939
And another thing that remains the same across
the board?

00:01:57.939 --> 00:02:00.049
The Leprechaun’s profession.

00:02:00.049 --> 00:02:02.790
This fairy creature is a shoemaker.

00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:07.369
Hence the tiny leather apron seen in most
traditional representations.

00:02:07.369 --> 00:02:10.240
&nbsp;
So where do we see variety in the leprechaun?

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:13.060
Well, for one thing, his odd attire.

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:18.670
In some regions he&nbsp; wears “old-style clothes,”
with one or more items of red clothing, buckles

00:02:18.670 --> 00:02:25.730
on his shoes, and a magical hat that allows
him to disappear at will and travel long distances.

00:02:25.730 --> 00:02:30.890
Other accounts have him rocking an Elizabethan
ruff and lace frills at his wrists.

00:02:30.890 --> 00:02:35.120
On the west coast, he wears an overcoat to
protect against the cold weather.

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:40.099
In Leinster they wear a green suit and hat
with a wide belt buckle and boots—which

00:02:40.099 --> 00:02:44.069
is likely where the modern version of the
leprechaun gets its look from.

00:02:44.069 --> 00:02:46.280
&nbsp;
Many experts agree that two other solitary

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:51.180
fairies of Celtic lore, the Clurican, and
the Far Darrig, are actually just variants

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:52.750
of the leprechaun.

00:02:52.750 --> 00:02:57.780
The Clurican is known for stealing liquor
from cellars and becoming very drunk, and

00:02:57.780 --> 00:03:01.519
thus you could say it is just a leprechaun
on a boozy binge.

00:03:01.519 --> 00:03:07.280
The Far Darrig, or Red Man, is a practical
jokester, a Leprechaun in red with a bit of

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:08.540
a mean streak.

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:14.090
So, about that cobbling…the leprechaun are
renowned for their work crafting beautiful

00:03:14.090 --> 00:03:15.090
shoes.

00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:18.290
They even make dancing slippers for the other
fairies.

00:03:18.290 --> 00:03:23.180
In fact, while conducting his research of
folklore in Ireland, William Butler Yeats

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:28.709
was told that the creature’s name comes
from leith brog meaning “One-shoemaker.”

00:03:28.709 --> 00:03:30.819
&nbsp;
Expert cobbling is a lucrative trade, and

00:03:30.819 --> 00:03:33.540
so leprechauns are very wealthy.

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:38.750
This leads to their other great and familiar
passion: being fiercely protective of their

00:03:38.750 --> 00:03:40.190
gold!

00:03:40.190 --> 00:03:44.560
Leprechauns are said to have caches of gold
hidden across Ireland, and they always carry

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:47.190
a full purse full of gold coins.

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:51.800
Using charms and craftiness, they horde their
treasures in mountains, woods, caves, and

00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:53.040
rocks.

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:55.450
Another thing the Leprechaun is reportedly
fond of?

00:03:55.450 --> 00:04:01.450
Throwing furniture around when frustrated—they
are apparently small but mighty, which…

00:04:01.450 --> 00:04:02.650
feels personally familiar.

00:04:02.650 --> 00:04:07.830
They are known to steal food, and when feeling
a bit lazy, sometimes they’ll hop on the

00:04:07.830 --> 00:04:10.069
back of a sheep to get around.

00:04:10.069 --> 00:04:12.230
They do grant wishes, which is why they are

00:04:12.230 --> 00:04:16.620
associated with luck, but they always demand
something in return.

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:18.729
&nbsp;
Take the story “Master and Man” from Thomas

00:04:18.729 --> 00:04:23.949
Crofton Croker’s 1825 Fairy legends and traditions of the south of Ireland.

00:04:23.949 --> 00:04:26.539
&nbsp;Billy, a man who loved his drink and a good

00:04:26.539 --> 00:04:31.439
fight, is returning home on the night of a
full moon when he wishes he had “a drop

00:04:31.439 --> 00:04:35.430
of good liquor…to keep a man’s soul from freezing in him.”

00:04:35.430 --> 00:04:40.479
Suddenly, “a little man in a three-cornered hat, bound all about with gold lace, and with

00:04:40.479 --> 00:04:46.550
great silver buckles in his shoes” holds
out a glass as large as he is and says “Never

00:04:46.550 --> 00:04:48.639
wish it twice, Billy.”

00:04:48.639 --> 00:04:53.979
Billy happily accepts the drink and downs
it, but then the Leprechaun demands payment.

00:04:53.979 --> 00:04:58.630
Billy insults the Leprechaun, who angrily
declares “you shall be my servant for seven

00:04:58.630 --> 00:05:02.080
years and a day, and that is the way I will
be paid.”

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:06.720
He warns Billy that if he fails to meet him
the next night, his situation will be even

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:07.869
worse.

00:05:07.869 --> 00:05:12.789
Billy heeds this warning, and ends up serving the Leprechaun for years with little question.

00:05:12.789 --> 00:05:14.949
His duties include a lot of magical breaking

00:05:14.949 --> 00:05:20.270
and entering, helping the Leprechaun drink wine from the cellars of rich men—so maybe

00:05:20.270 --> 00:05:23.099
the Leprechaun wanted a drinking buddy?

00:05:23.099 --> 00:05:24.919
While leprechauns can imprison humans for

00:05:24.919 --> 00:05:30.389
their advantage, a human can also reciprocate,
capturing a leprechaun to steal his gold by

00:05:30.389 --> 00:05:32.979
exchanging his release for the treasure.

00:05:32.979 --> 00:05:36.539
However, this exchange sounds easier than it is.

00:05:36.539 --> 00:05:41.119
Confronted with a choice between his freedom and his precious riches, the wily leprechaun

00:05:41.119 --> 00:05:45.889
may offer his captor three wishes instead of the loot, or some other temptation.

00:05:45.889 --> 00:05:50.369
If the human captor falls for it, they find
themselves up against a confounding riddle

00:05:50.369 --> 00:05:55.149
or ruse as the means to the wishes, and more
often than not will be tricked into freeing

00:05:55.149 --> 00:05:58.819
the leprechaun, losing out on both treasure and wishes.

00:05:58.819 --> 00:06:01.830
Sort of like in the story “The Little Shoe.”

00:06:01.830 --> 00:06:05.380
The story tells of a man who once caught a Leprechaun.

00:06:05.380 --> 00:06:08.030
One day after cutting peat, he returns to
his stable.

00:06:08.030 --> 00:06:13.689
When he gets to the stable door he hears hammering and someone whistling “the prettiest tune

00:06:13.689 --> 00:06:16.719
he ever heard in his whole life before.”

00:06:16.719 --> 00:06:20.689
He thinks it is a Cluricaune, or Leprechaun, and decides to catch him.

00:06:20.689 --> 00:06:26.479
He opens the door silently and peers around, locating the “little fellow” under a horse,

00:06:26.479 --> 00:06:30.460
wearing an apron and sporting a red nightcap, making a shoe.

00:06:30.460 --> 00:06:35.580
The shoemaker is so involved in the work that he fails to notice the human, who catches

00:06:35.580 --> 00:06:37.470
him with his bare hands.

00:06:37.470 --> 00:06:39.990
The man demands the Leprechaun’s purse.

00:06:39.990 --> 00:06:45.379
The Leprechaun says he will go get it for the man, who foolishly loosens his hand, allowing

00:06:45.379 --> 00:06:49.479
the little cobbler to escape, laughing all
the while.

00:06:49.479 --> 00:06:54.509
But he left the little shoe behind and the man took it, reveling in its tiny beauty for

00:06:54.509 --> 00:06:56.019
the rest of his life.

00:06:56.019 --> 00:06:58.879
This story also comes from Croker’s collection;

00:06:58.879 --> 00:07:04.229
the author and folklorist would later argue that Leprechauns, in all their variations,

00:07:04.229 --> 00:07:07.039
are what the Irish call pygmy peoples.

00:07:07.039 --> 00:07:11.300
Could he be saying that these legends are based on real people of shorter stature?

00:07:11.300 --> 00:07:15.559
Well, there isn’t any archaeological evidence to suggest that’s true.&nbsp;

00:07:15.559 --> 00:07:18.139
But speaking of the origins of leprechauns,

00:07:18.139 --> 00:07:23.679
the earliest written reference appears long before Croker’s seminal work, in the 7th

00:07:23.679 --> 00:07:27.449
or 8th-century text [Echtra Fergus mac Léti] The Adventure of Fergus Son of Leti.

00:07:27.449 --> 00:07:32.059
In this medieval story, a king falls asleep on a beach and awakens to find himself being

00:07:32.059 --> 00:07:35.469
dragged into the ocean by three leprechauns.

00:07:35.469 --> 00:07:39.999
He manages to turn the tables and capture the creatures himself, and they agree to grant

00:07:39.999 --> 00:07:42.539
him three wishes in exchange for their release.

00:07:42.539 --> 00:07:47.499
Then there’s the story of the Fir Bolg,
a legendary race of small people who invaded

00:07:47.499 --> 00:07:50.800
Ireland after fleeing persecution in Greece.

00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:55.139
According to myth, they were born from Nemed’s son Starn, and they became some of the first

00:07:55.139 --> 00:07:58.069
inhabitants to rule over Ireland.

00:07:58.069 --> 00:08:00.089
Their reign was short-lived however, as less

00:08:00.089 --> 00:08:05.569
than 40 years later the Fir Bolg were conquered by the Tuatha Dé Danann, a mighty supernatural

00:08:05.569 --> 00:08:06.649
race.

00:08:06.649 --> 00:08:11.369
According to Celtic mythology, the Tuatha De Danann are skilled in magic, and were banished

00:08:11.369 --> 00:08:14.169
from the heavens because of their great knowledge.

00:08:14.169 --> 00:08:19.449
They built grand palaces underground, rumored to contain unimaginable treasures that very

00:08:19.449 --> 00:08:22.129
few humans would ever get to see.

00:08:22.129 --> 00:08:27.879
Considered immortal gods, the Tuatha are not just part of the foundation of Irish mythology,

00:08:27.879 --> 00:08:33.500
but are actually representative of the intersection between folklore and history itself.

00:08:33.500 --> 00:08:38.570
After all, the story goes on to tell of a
battle between the Tuatha and the very people

00:08:38.570 --> 00:08:41.750
who would become the ancestors of the modern Irish.

00:08:41.750 --> 00:08:43.909
So are they legend, or history?

00:08:43.909 --> 00:08:46.750
In fact, magical beings like the Fihr Bolg,

00:08:46.750 --> 00:08:51.550
the Tuatha Dé Danann , and the leprechaun, are featured throughout Irish folklore, leading

00:08:51.550 --> 00:08:54.660
to this conflation of myth and reality.

00:08:54.660 --> 00:09:00.410
For example, until as late as the 17th century, the Tuatha de Danann were regarded as a true

00:09:00.410 --> 00:09:01.410
race.

00:09:01.410 --> 00:09:06.240
So we’ve talked about the hard-working jokester leprechaun who protects his gold and can grant

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:11.940
wishes, but what about the more malicious, drunken fellow sowing chaos...and stealing

00:09:11.940 --> 00:09:12.940
babies?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.940 --> 00:09:17.790
Well, around the start of the 5th century CE, as Christianity became the dominant religion

00:09:17.790 --> 00:09:23.411
in Ireland, the ancient, pagan, stories of—not to mention, actual belief in—fairies like

00:09:23.411 --> 00:09:25.290
the leprechaun still lingered.

00:09:25.290 --> 00:09:30.370
However, over time, these impish creatures are cast in an increasingly negative light,

00:09:30.370 --> 00:09:34.950
the stories becoming darker and the creatures more menacing, until we begin to see tales

00:09:34.950 --> 00:09:39.829
in which leprechauns would replace babies with changelings (fairy children) or murder

00:09:39.829 --> 00:09:44.370
potential treasure thieves rather than just trick or deter them.&nbsp;

00:09:44.370 --> 00:09:48.610
Outside of Ireland, the popularity of the
leprechaun rose later, in the late 19th and

00:09:48.610 --> 00:09:53.589
early 20th centuries, most notably because of its appearance in Thomas Crofton Croker’s

00:09:53.589 --> 00:09:56.399
widely admired collection of Irish lore.

00:09:56.399 --> 00:09:59.399
But there were other contributing factors as well.

00:09:59.399 --> 00:10:01.339
&nbsp;Beginning in the mid-19th century, as the

00:10:01.339 --> 00:10:05.689
Irish fled their famine-stricken homeland
to England and the United States, we see an

00:10:05.689 --> 00:10:10.850
infusion of leprechaun lore and notoriety in the folk stories of those countries.

00:10:10.850 --> 00:10:12.700
Treatment of the Irish immigrants and Irish

00:10:12.700 --> 00:10:18.250
Americans during this influx of immigration was dismal at best and downright atrocious

00:10:18.250 --> 00:10:19.730
at its worst.

00:10:19.730 --> 00:10:24.509
While Celtic lore positions the leprechaun as a skilled cobbler who amassed great wealth

00:10:24.509 --> 00:10:30.399
from their exquisite craftsmanship, as anti-Irish prejudice grew, non-Irish people began to

00:10:30.399 --> 00:10:35.790
associate the leprechaun with less complimentary, stereotypical representations.

00:10:35.790 --> 00:10:41.540
The leprechaun became synonymous with prejudices of the Irish as drunken, irresponsible happy

00:10:41.540 --> 00:10:46.790
people, blithely and even joyfully indulging their greed, always on the lookout for luck

00:10:46.790 --> 00:10:48.550
rather than hard work.

00:10:48.550 --> 00:10:54.580
So how and why did the leprechaun become associated with St. Patrick’s Day, a holiday that initially

00:10:54.580 --> 00:10:59.420
celebrated the death of a saint and resulted in raucous celebrations across the globe?&nbsp;

00:10:59.420 --> 00:11:04.660
While there is a 1,000 year history of celebrating the holiday with food and drink, the first

00:11:04.660 --> 00:11:11.660
St. Patrick’s Day parade actually occurred
on American soil on March 17, 1772 when homesick

00:11:11.660 --> 00:11:17.570
Irish soldiers serving British forces marched in New York City in honor of the saint.

00:11:17.570 --> 00:11:22.550
It only exploded from there, especially with the influx of immigrants from the 19th century

00:11:22.550 --> 00:11:23.759
on.&nbsp;

00:11:23.759 --> 00:11:27.760
As for leprechauns serving as a mythic symbol
of the day, that’s harder to pin down.&nbsp;

00:11:27.760 --> 00:11:30.490
Although it likely has something to do with
consumerism.

00:11:30.490 --> 00:11:36.050
Today, due in part to the efforts of Enterprise
Ireland, an Irish government entity that promotes

00:11:36.050 --> 00:11:42.440
trade of Irish goods, and North American Celtic
retailers, the demand for Irish adjacent consumer

00:11:42.440 --> 00:11:44.899
items, has boomed since the late 1980s.

00:11:44.899 --> 00:11:50.139
Then there’s the increased interest from Irish Americans in discovering their ancestral

00:11:50.139 --> 00:11:55.370
origins--something DNA testing and sites like Ancestry.com make easier.

00:11:55.370 --> 00:12:01.639
In some ways the leprechaun is the entry point into Irishness--a recognizable figure&nbsp; of

00:12:01.639 --> 00:12:06.499
Ireland in popular culture, despite modern differences from ancient Irish folklore.&nbsp;

00:12:06.499 --> 00:12:08.680
&nbsp;
The story of the leprechaun has continued

00:12:08.680 --> 00:12:13.620
to evolve, and today we tell children of a jaunty little green man with a buckled hat

00:12:13.620 --> 00:12:18.050
and a red beard, just waiting on the other side of the rainbow to happily hand over his

00:12:18.050 --> 00:12:22.909
pot of gold, if you can find it—that’s
the story I was told.

00:12:22.909 --> 00:12:25.199
Magical shoemaker, drunken trickster, violent

00:12:25.199 --> 00:12:26.620
child-stealer.

00:12:26.620 --> 00:12:30.510
The leprechaun has played all these roles, and more.

00:12:30.510 --> 00:12:35.089
But next time you raise a pint of green beer
just remember that ultimately leprechauns

00:12:35.089 --> 00:12:54.970
are about hard work—they earned their riches,
and they know how to protect them.

